// ADR 0112 §4, ADR 0124 §6: what a lock written in fiig provides. A program
// never calls it: the runtime claims a firing before running its workflow,
// and a startup route that says `once` before running its check. claim is
// true for exactly one caller per job and firedAt; the claim lasts
// holdMillis. complete records that the claimant finished, for retainMillis,
// and completed asks whether anyone has, so a replica that lost the claim can
// wait for the winner instead of starting early. A schedule only claims; a
// startup route uses all three, with a deploy's id as firedAt. A lock's
// manifest names the group that fulfils it:
// "provides": {"fiig.schedule.ScheduleLock": "RedisScheduleLock"}.
interface ScheduleLock {
    claim(job: string, firedAt: int, holder: string, holdMillis: int) -> Result<bool, LockError>
    complete(job: string, firedAt: int, holder: string, retainMillis: int) -> Result<bool, LockError>
    completed(job: string, firedAt: int) -> Result<bool, LockError>
}
